module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.emitted_grammar_optional_absent_case_diverges_from_the_interpreter

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { CurrentRungUnstatedAfterClimb, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data emitted_grammar_optional_absent_case_diverges_from_the_interpreter: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "emitted_grammar_optional_absent_case_diverges_from_the_interpreter" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: CurrentRungUnstatedAfterClimb },

  receipts: [
    "**the emitted realization of an optional grammar rule's ABSENT case diverges from the interpreted .dag on one input** (INVALID STATE: one source text is parsed by TWO realizations of the SAME canonical .dag parse function -- parse_module_prepared_seeded -- and they give DIFFERENT verdicts. Under the interpreter (the native test route, native_test_context_from_ingest -> native_census_module_resolution) the text PARSES and refuses only later at LOWERING with else_less_if_unlowered. Under the emitted gen-1 binary (the native CLI acquisition route, acquisition_parsed -> parse_module_prepared -> parse_module_prepared_seeded) the SAME text refuses at PARSE with parse_g0_tokens_remain. The text is a module whose single fn body is an ELSE-LESS if followed by a tail statement: `fn f(c: Bool, i: Int) -> Int {` / `  if c { i }` / `  i` / `}` (the rtp_guard_fallthrough_source fixture, byte-identical across the three cc2-ceprobe runs, member file sha256 0f785394e803ce694370e7c917d7faad069b70bfabc2eb2167cd2160dcf409a8; emit.err sha256 837b2a71). The interpreter is RIGHT: the grammar production dag_grammar_if_block_form is `seq(fn_body, optional(if_else_tail))` (dag.dag:2414), so an else-less `if c { i }` is a valid block-form member and the trailing `i` is the next statement; the whole module parses and the else-less refusal belongs at lowering).",

    "**THE EARLIEST UNJUSTIFIED LINK IS THE EMITTED `optional(...)` ABSENT CASE** (REASONING: the module production is a sequence(), which accepts only when EVERY element accepts; and parse_expr_repeat (parse.dag:4286) ALWAYS returns Accepted. So the only way a well-formed module can reach `accepted but remaining non-empty -> parse_g0_tokens_remain` is if, on this input, the emitted realization of the optional's absent case MIS-SELECTS its arm -- the interpreter's parse_expr_optional (parse.dag:4195) turns a rejected element into Accepted with the token stream UNCHANGED (`Rejected { diagnostics: _, table } => Accepted { remaining: tokens, captured: grammar_empty_node(), diagnostics: [], prov: prov, table }`). If that absent arm instead lets the element's rejection escape, if_block_form dies, BOTH alternatives of the if_expr choice die, the fn production rejects, the module's items repeat accepts ZERO items and returns with the whole fn unconsumed, and the module sequence accepts with a non-empty remainder. A WHOLESALE arm drop is ruled out: every absent optional in the language (every optional comma, where-clause, etc.) would then reject, and the emitted compiler demonstrably parses thousands of modules. So the fault is arm SELECTION specific to a rejected element, not the arm's existence). This is the guard-vs-discriminant boundary of the nested_pattern_accepted_by_the_interpreter_and_broken_in_emitted_rust family: emit_typed_match_arm (05_emit_rust.dag:15698) emits a PLAIN coproduct arm as a correct discriminant match with NO guard, and only routes an arm through the guard machinery (collect_pattern_rc_variant_guards / rc_pattern_preludes) when analyze_rc_pattern marks it needs_rc_pattern -- the accepted/present and rejected/absent arms of ParseExprResult bind shared fields (captured: GrammarNode, table: ParseTable) and reach that path.",

    "**WHAT MAKES IT RECURRING** (all 49 dag_grammar_optional grammar sites in the language surface share ONE lowering, so one wrong arm decision is one class, not one grammar rule): every optional(...) in the grammar is built by dag_grammar_optional -> grammar_expr_optional -> PreparedOptional and is parsed by the SAME parse_expr_optional (interpreted) / its single emitted realization. A wrong absent-case decision therefore threatens every grammar-optional (dag.dag:938 wrapper and the ~49 call sites: type-parameter suffix, class/trait item flags, expr/stmt optionals, refinement/guard tails, etc.). Whether a given site PRODUCES a wrong VERDICT depends on grammar shape -- it must be an optional whose element can reject inside a prefix that a surrounding sequence-with-repeat can then accept-with-remainder; the else-less if is exactly such a shape, which is why it is the discriminating input and why no well-formed module in the corpus exposes it.",

    "**WHY IT IS NOT AN ABSENCE OF COVERAGE** (the interpreter is RIGHT, so no interpreted claim reddens -- the same blind spot the freemonoid list_init miscompile had, whose fix comment records 'THE INTERPRETER LOWERS ALL OF THESE CORRECTLY, which is why no interpreted claim discriminates the class'). A text-match emitted witness (compile_dag_rust_emit_check) CANNOT distinguish the two verdicts, because BOTH the wrong and the right realization compile and emit; per the nested_pattern row's N7-1 rule a text check 'cannot tell the two apart, so a probe written with it would be a decoration.' The only witness that discriminates is a BUILT-RUN differential: the same source text run through the interpreter AND through the freshly-emitted gen-1 binary, asserting one parse verdict.",

    "**RUNG FOUND AT** rung 1 -- one observed divergence between the two realizations on one real grammar input, reproduced three times on srv2 (cc2-ceprobe / ceprobe2 / ceprobe3, at main 0dc96e1e, seed gunbc 9e5ee601, gen-1 CLI 3ffe505e), with the interpreted control (namespace_xl0) green and the emitted side red. No interpreter claim discriminates it, and there is no per-input emitted differential in the durable set yet.",

    "**TRIGGER** the both-executor discriminating probe: build the seed gunbc, emit the gen-1 (gunbc compile --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry src/v2/cli/compile_cli.dag --target rust), build the emitted crate, and run BOTH executors on the verbatim else-less member, expecting the SAME verdict (parse-OK -> refuse at lowering with else_less_if_unlowered). Red while the emitted absent case is wrong; green once the emitted realization of optional's absent case matches the interpreter.",

    "**RECOGNITION RULE** wherever a construct is realized independently by the interpreter and by the emission target, ask what fact decides its behaviour and where that fact is DECLARED; for an optional of a grammar rule the deciding fact is the element's parse RESULT (accepted vs rejected), and the absent case is DECLARED in parse_expr_optional as 'rejected element => Accepted with the token stream unchanged'. Any emitted arm that decides that case on anything other than the parse-result discriminant -- on a field compare, an rc guard that reads only the outermost variant shape, or an arm order that lets a present-value arm win -- is a candidate for this class. Do NOT special-case one grammar rule (do not name if_else_tail): the class is the absent-case arm of the shared optional lowering.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.test.claim.namespace_xl0.return_tail_position", decl_name: "an_else_less_if_that_falls_through_still_refuses_as_unlowered", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.test.claim.namespace_xl0.return_tail_position", decl_name: "an_else_less_if_as_the_last_statement_still_refuses_as_unlowered", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
